Subject: [PATCH 10218/11362] Queue exp6716: the object-table token/quality
 trade as an A/B, not an optimisation

Pre-staged for milestone 2026.08.586 per the Pre-Staged Roadmap Convention.

The object table is the last large token lever in the induce prompt -- 4,667 of
8,678 tokens, about 54 percent, independently reproduced at 4,691 / 55.5 on a
different seed. Frames are not the lever: keyframe-plus-delta shipped months
ago, a raw grid is 4,159 tokens against 1,709 encoded, and palette schemes buy
nothing because tokens already equal characters 1:1 on digit-heavy text.

But the table CANNOT simply be cut. It is default-ON because of a measured
held-out win: +0.0720 change_fidelity, 20 paired games, sign-test p = 0.0192,
19 of 20 discordant, 13,171 seconds of compute. A design note recommended
fetch-on-demand and an independent re-derivation overturned that
recommendation on exactly those grounds; the task prompt sends the reader to
the CORRECTION before the note.

So the gate is pre-registered as a defence of the existing result rather than a
token target: fetch-on-demand is adopted ONLY if change_fidelity stays at least
flat against the noise floor the 2026-08-01 artifact recorded in its own
NOISE_FLOOR_within_arm_replicate_spread field. A loss is a result, and the
prompt says so -- if the table earns its tokens, the direction closes.

Two things the task must report that a naive version would miss. How often the
model actually CALLS find_objects when the table is absent, because a
fetch-on-demand arm that never fetches is an ablation rather than a token win.
And that this is the FIRST live code-carrying tool call: the selfparse gate
passed 20/20, but all 20 live turns were the zero-argument list_transitions, so
the multi-parameter shape is so far proven only on a stored emission and unit
fixtures.

Preconditions include lowering CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_N_CTX -- the pooled default
needs ~26.6 GiB, the guard declines a 24 GiB card, and the generator silently
falls back to the ~2 tok/s iGPU.

